[38;5;231m⏺[39m Committed: team tool. That means sub-project #1 isn't just "tasks" — it's tasks plus enough of a server to own them.
  Notifications in step 4 will want a persistent connection (SSE or WebSocket) and a durable event log, so the runtime
  we pick now has to make those easy rather than bolted on.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the language and runtime for the server. Nothing exists yet, so this is free to choose and expensive to
  switch once there's a schema and a test suite.

  - [1mPython + FastAPI + SQLite[0m — matches the toolchain your [38;5;153mCLAUDE.md[39m documents in depth (micromamba/uv, ruff, mypy, RST
    docstrings), so lint/type/test conventions come for free. SSE is straightforward. Costs: the browser side stays
    hand-written JS unless we add a build step later.
  - [1mNode or Bun + SQLite[0m — one language across server and browser, SSE is trivial, single process, trivial deploy.
    Costs: your documented tooling conventions don't apply, so we'd establish new ones.
  - [1mFull-stack JS framework[0m (Next.js / SvelteKit) — auth, routing, and data loading are largely solved problems
    in-framework. Costs: significant machinery on top of what is currently an 11-line HTML file, and framework
    conventions will drive the design more than our spec does.

  I recommend [1mPython + FastAPI + SQLite[0m. Your environment and quality standards are already written down for Python, the
  data model here is small, and SQLite keeps step 3's event log to a table rather than a service.
